  "dom_id": "step-q43081",
  "name": "Question 25",
  "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=-108$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by multiplying the $2$nd row by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the determinant is multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-108) \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
